开始涉足一个大多数巴勒斯坦人在学校里从未接触过的话题——大屠杀。但穆罕默德的学生们沉浸在他们自己的创伤中，不能也不愿倾听。他的一个学生是一个叫哈南（Hanan）的年轻女性。她曾在以色列监狱服刑九年。所以对她来说，她看着犹太人在奥斯威辛、克拉科夫、比克瑙以及不同集中营里发生的事情，就像是把这些投射到她自己作为囚犯在以色列监狱中以及作为女性遭受歧视的经历上。

25
00:14:38.000 --> 00:15:17.000
being a prisoner in Israeli jails and being discriminated against as a woman. Muhammad told Hanan she had constructed a prison inside her own mind, a prison that kept her from seeing the world from the point of view of her enemy. When nothing worked with the students, Muhammad decided he would take Hanan and a couple dozen other students on a field trip to Europe. They would visit Nazi concentration camps and learn, first hand, about the trauma at the heart of the Israeli Jewish experience. It was a scientific project to study about empathy, about if you learn about the suffering
穆罕默德告诉哈南，她在自己的心灵内部建造了一座监狱，一座让她无法从敌人角度看世界的监狱。当对学生们的所有努力都无济于事时，穆罕默德决定带着哈南和其他几十名学生去欧洲进行一次实地考察。他们将参观纳粹集中营，亲身体验以色列犹太人经历核心的创伤。这是一个研究同理心的科学项目，旨在探讨如果你了解对方的痛苦，这是否有助于促进你的和解态度。我们决定带着这 30 名巴勒斯坦学生前往波兰的奥斯威辛和克拉科夫。几乎立刻，麻烦就来了。

26
00:15:17.000 --> 00:16:05.000
of the other, would that help more in your attitude for reconciliation. We decided to take these 30 Palestinian students to Auschwitz and Krakow in Poland. Almost immediately, there was trouble. So I received an email from the president of the university. He said that I heard rumors that you are taking students to Auschwitz, and I would like you to make it very clear to them that the university has nothing to do with this trip. Another student came to me and said, you should cancel this trip, and that if you would go once you come back, you might be facing a very big threat.
我收到了大学校长的电子邮件。他说我听到谣言说你要带学生去奥斯威辛，我希望你向他们明确表示，学校与这次旅行毫无关系。另一个学生来找我说，你应该取消这次旅行，如果你去了，等你回来，你可能会面临巨大的威胁。

27
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:53.000
Muhammad did not waver. He was an educator. And like Avner, he believed he was being a patriot. Empathy he had come to believe was the only way to understanding and to peace. When they got to Auschwitz, Muhammad and Hanan stopped at the entrance to the camp. And then when she saw the, at the entrance, the, there was like a blackout saying, Arbite-Macht-Frey, the, so works at Setsu-Frey. So she was very surprised with that slogan on the at the gate of the aggregate of the Auschwitz camp and asked me about it and I said, why don't you lock it up?
穆罕默德没有动摇。他是一位教育家。和阿夫纳一样，他坚信自己是一名爱国者。他渐渐坚信，同理心是通往理解和和平的唯一途径。当他们到达奥斯威辛时，穆罕默德和哈南在集中营入口处停了下来。然后当她看到入口处，有一个像黑板一样的牌子，上面写着 Arbite-Macht-Frey，也就是劳动带来自由。所以她对奥斯威辛集中营大门上的这个标语感到非常惊讶，并问我关于它的事，我说，你为什么不去读读看呢？

28
00:16:53.000 --> 00:17:48.000
Hanan learned the meaning of the cynical phrase. The Jewish prisoners who entered this camp were not set free by work. They were worked to death or killed. There was this big room which had toilets and we were told that every morning more than 2,000 people gathered in front of the room and then they make them go through there to use it and they are given ten seconds to use it and then they have to leave. And so it was such a humiliation for the individual. They were things they never heard of in terms of how human beings were humiliated and broken. As they walked through the camp, Muhammad noticed that Hanan and the other students were
哈南懂得了这个讽刺短语的含义。进入这个集中营的犹太囚犯并没有通过劳动获得自由。他们被劳累致死或被杀害。这里有一个大房间，里面有厕所，我们被告知每天早上有 2000 多人聚集在房间前，然后他们排队进去使用，只给他们十秒钟的时间使用，之后他们就必须离开。这对个人来说是极大的羞辱。这些事情是他们从未听说过的，关于人类是如何被羞辱和摧毁的。当他们穿过集中营时，穆罕默德注意到哈南和其他学生

29
00:17:48.000 --> 00:18:51.000
no longer seeing the Holocaust through the lens of their own suffering. They were actually empathizing with Jews, their enemies. It was very emotional to them, one of the, and some of the, some of the girls even cried. One of the students was telling me, I thought that Hitler gathered Jews in these concentration camps to send them to Palestine, to have them ship to Palestine. So to them, they had a total misconception about what is the Holocaust, what is the concentration camp, what did it mean, how life was there. And so it was an open out for them. But back at home, Muhammad's colleagues at the university did not see the trip as an invaluable educational experience.
不再透过自身苦难的有色眼镜来看待大屠杀。他们实际上在对犹太人——他们的敌人——产生同理心。这让他们情绪非常激动，其中一些女孩甚至哭了。其中一个学生告诉我，我以为希特勒把犹太人聚集在这些集中营里是为了把他们送到巴勒斯坦，把他们运送到巴勒斯坦。所以对他们来说，他们对什么是大屠杀、什么是集中营、它意味着什么、那里的生活是怎样的，有着完全错误的看法。因此，这对他们来说是一次大开眼界的经历。但回到家后，穆罕默德在大学的同事们并不认为这次旅行是一次无价的教育经历。

30
00:18:51.000 --> 00:19:53.000
The last day in Auschwitz, my secretary wrote to me an email saying that students have come in the Renshaktor office and they were making demonstrations against your own campus and they came and left you a letter of threatening your life that you should not come back to the university and that if you come back they are going to kill you. And the letter is with me and she said that there were extremely, there is a lot of enmity and an uproar on campus regarding the trip. So anyway, that prepared me that the reception will not be easy going back to the university. And once I went there, I noticed that there was a lot of enmity people were looking at me as if I have a
在奥斯威辛的最后一天，我的秘书给我写了一封电子邮件，说学生们去了校长办公室，他们正在校园里针对你进行示威游行，他们来并留下一封威胁你生命的信，说你不应该回到大学，如果你回来他们就会杀了你。信在我这里，她说校园里关于这次旅行有极度的敌意和轩然大波。无论如何，这让我做好准备，回到大学后受到的接待不会一帆风顺。一旦我去了那里，我注意到有很多敌意，人们看着我，就好像我是一个叛徒，背叛了他们一样。

31
00:19:53.000 --> 00:20:24.000
a betrayer, betrayed them. At the same time, nine student organizations issued a statement on campus saying that this is normalization and normalization equals treason. Muhammad was forced to resign from his job. One night someone set fire to his car. He knew his life was in danger. He packed his bags and left Jerusalem again. This time exiled by his fellow Palestinians.
与此同时，九个学生组织在校园里发表声明，称这是正常化，而正常化等于叛国。穆罕默德被迫辞职。一天晚上，有人放火烧了他的汽车。他知道自己的生命处于危险之中。他收拾行装，再次离开耶路撒冷。这一次，他被同胞巴勒斯坦人流放了。

32
00:20:24.000 --> 00:21:10.000
I asked him how it felt to be called a traitor. What was most helpful for me, most painful, was nobody stood by me. That was really what was most painful for me. Also the fact that I have dedicated all my life for the Palestinian cause and suddenly I'm a traitor for that cause, so that also was painful. When I talked to Muhammad, he was living in Washington, just like Avner, who is also in the United States, Muhammad hopes to go home someday.
我问他被叫做叛徒是什么感觉。对我而言，最伤人、最痛苦的是，没有人支持我。那真的是对我最痛苦的事。还有就是我把一生的心血都奉献给了巴勒斯坦事业，而突然之间我成了这一事业的叛徒，所以这也让人痛苦。当我采访穆罕默德时，他住在华盛顿，就像同样身在美国的阿夫纳一样，穆罕默德希望有一天能回家。

33
00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:51.000
I want to leave you with one last note. As I was learning the stories of Avner and Guariaho and Muhammad Dajani, I went to see a powerful new play called Wrestling Jerusalem. Commissioned by Ari Roth and produced by Mosaic Theater in Washington DC, wrestling Jerusalem is a one-man show that will travel to many parts of the country. Like Avner and Muhammad's real-life stories, the play is about the intertwined traumas of the Nakba and the Holocaust, and the effects those traumas have on the ability of Israelis and Palestinians to empathize with one another. Playwright and performer Aaron Davidman describes his travels in Israel and the occupied territories.
最后，我想留给您一句话。在了解阿夫纳·格瓦里亚胡和穆罕默德·达贾尼的故事时，我去看了部极具震撼力的新话剧，名为《摔跤耶路撒冷》（Wrestling Jerusalem）。由阿里·罗斯（Ari Roth）委约创作、华盛顿特区马赛克剧院（Mosaic Theater）制作的《摔跤耶路撒冷》是一部独角戏，将在该国许多地方巡演。就像阿夫纳和穆罕默德的真实生活故事一样，这部话剧讲述了“纳克巴”与大屠杀交织在一起的创伤，以及这些创伤对以色列人和巴勒斯坦人彼此产生同理心能力的影响。剧作家兼表演者亚伦·戴维曼（Aaron Davidman）描述了他在以色列和被占领土的旅行。

34
00:21:51.000 --> 00:22:40.000
You'll hear him speak as himself, and in the voices of Palestinians, and an Israeli man named Nadav. I'm looking for the number 18 bus in East Jerusalem, somewhere up the street from Damascus gate, the Arab entrance to the old city. It's only a few blocks from the Jewish part of Jerusalem, but it feels like another world. The smell of the food vendors, language in the air, gestures of the people in the street, all so different. I can't find the bus stop and have a nervous feeling of being on the other side. I've been to Israel many times. I've never been to Ramallah. When I finally find the small bus, I pay six shekels and I get on. I sit in the back. I'm sure I'm the only Jewish person on the bus, but I feel invisible.
您将听到他以自己的声音，以及巴勒斯坦人和一个名叫纳达夫（Nadav）的以色列男子的声音发言。我在东耶路撒冷寻找 18 路公交车，在距离老城阿拉伯入口大马士革门不远的一条街上。它距离耶路撒冷犹太人区只有几个街区之隔，但感觉就像另一个世界。小贩食物的气味、空气中的语言、街上人们的手势，都是如此不同。我找不到公交车站，有一种身处敌方阵营的紧张感。我去过以色列很多次。我从未去过拉姆安拉。当我终于找到那辆小型公交车时，我付了六谢克尔并上了车。我坐在后面。我确信我是车上唯一的犹太人，但我感觉自己像隐形了一样。

35
00:22:40.000 --> 00:23:28.000
No one seems to pay any attention to me. The atmosphere is quiet, tired. As soon as we get on the highway, the bus is stopped by the Israeli police. Tadut, vaksha! I clutch my passport. I don't want to pass it forward. I just sit there. The officer doesn't see me. Now my invisibility feels conspicuous. There's a blank stare in the eyes of the middle-aged Palestinian men as they pass their IDs forward. Do you speak English? A woman turns to me. Do they ask to see your ID? No. You see how they humiliate us. Ten minutes later, we approach the Kalandia checkpoint. There's a long line of cars and people on foot waiting to cross through.
似乎没有人注意到我。气氛安静而疲惫。我们一上高速公路，公交车就被以色列警察拦了下来。“Tadut, vaksha！”我紧紧抓着护照。我不想把它往前递。我只是坐在那里。警察没有看到我。现在我的隐形感让人觉得格外显眼。中年巴勒斯坦人把身份证往前递时，眼中透出茫然的凝视。“你会说英语吗？”一个女人转向我。“他们要求看你的身份证吗？”“没有。你看他们是怎么羞辱我们的。”十分钟后，我们接近了卡兰迪亚检查站。排着长队的汽车和步行等待过境的人绵延不绝。

36
00:23:28.000 --> 00:24:13.000
Manned by the Israeli Army, Kalandia is one of the few places to cross from Israel into the West Bank and from the West Bank into Israel. Hundreds of kilometers of electric fences, trenches, barbed wire, and eight meter high concrete walls run north and south of this crossing, creating a de facto border. But Selam, the Israeli human rights organization, calls this structure the separation barrier. The Israelis call it the security fence, the Palestinians call it the apartheid wall. Whatever you want to call it, it's massive and intimidating and haunting. The Kalandia Crossing is a rocky, no-man's land, with two giant ominous watchtowers surrounded
由以色列军队把守的卡兰迪亚是从以色列进入约旦河西岸以及从约旦河西岸进入以色列的少数几个过境点之一。数公里的电网、战壕、铁丝网以及八米高的混凝土墙在这处过境点的南北两侧延伸，构成了事实上的边界。但是以色列人权组织“贝采莱姆”（B'Tselem）将这一建筑称为隔离墙。以色列人称之为安全隔离墙，巴勒斯坦人称之为种族隔离墙。不管你想怎么称呼它，它是庞大、威严而又挥之不去的。卡兰迪亚过境处是一片岩石密布的无人区，有两个巨大的、不祥的岗楼，四周环绕着缠绕着铁丝网的栅栏。

37
00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:50.000
by fences laced with barbed wire. Concentration camps. All I can think of is concentration camps. Concentration camps. A lifetime of Holocaust imagery comes lurching forward from the back of my mind. No! Don't think of concentration camps. There's no moral equivalency here. It's not the same. It's not the same guard towers, guard dogs, barbed wire, soldiers, machine guns, refugees, suitcases. Now the Jews are the guards and I'm on the train going into the camps and I I I I
集中营。我脑子里想的全都是集中营。集中营。一生中积累的大屠杀意象从我脑海深处猛地涌现出来。不！不要想集中营。这里没有道德对等。这不是一回事。不是一回事——岗楼、军犬、铁丝网、士兵、机枪、难民、手提箱。现在犹太人成了看守，而我在驶入集中营的火车上，我，我，我，我，我……

